From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] RedfishConfigHandler: quiesce the Redfish stack after provisioning

Hot-unplugging the NIC that carries the Redfish service is fatal after the
feature drivers have bound to it. Observed on RPi5 (2026-08-17) with the
BMC's USB CDC-NCM gadget re-enumerating while the user sat in Setup:

  UsbBusDxe detach -> DisconnectController cascade (NetworkCommon, Snp,
  Mnp, Arp, Ip4, Tcp teardown) -> destroying RedfishDiscoverDxe's TCP4
  probe child re-enters its Stop(), which DisconnectControllers the
  discover-protocol handle mid-cascade -> RedfishConfigDriverBindingStop
  sweeps every registered EDKII_REDFISH_CONFIG_HANDLER's Stop() -> each
  RedfishClientPkg feature driver calls RedfishCleanupService() on a
  service whose HTTP child died earlier in the same cascade ->
  HttpIoDestroyIo's Http->Configure(Http, NULL) branches through the freed
  EFI_HTTP_PROTOCOL instance:

    Synchronous Exception at 0xAFAFAFAFAFAFAFAF   (DEBUG freed-pool fill)
    X0 = stale Http pointer, X1 = 0 (NULL config), X2 = 0xAF.. fn pointer

The feature drivers hold their HTTP/REST EX references without driver-model
opens, so the handle database cannot order the teardown for them.

Fix: stop every config handler while the transport is still fully alive
(RedfishCleanupService then runs against valid protocol instances and NULLs
the service pointers - the handlers' Stop() is idempotent, so the later
sweep from a real disconnect cascade finds nothing to free), and latch
gRedfishConfigQuiesced so the config-handler driver never initializes
handlers or re-acquires services past that point. The latch also keeps the
cached gEfiRedfishDiscoverProtocol from being dereferenced when a
re-enumerated NIC reinstalls the discover protocol after its old instance
was freed.

The trigger has to be chosen carefully. ReadyToBoot is the obvious one and
is wrong whenever RedfishClientPkg is also built: its feature core
provisions from a ReadyToBoot callback of its own, and the two callbacks
sit in the same event group at the same TPL with nothing defining their
order. In practice this driver's callback runs FIRST - the DXE core queues
signal-list entries with InsertHeadList so the later-created event notifies
earlier, and this driver is always dispatched after the feature core
because of its dependency on the credential protocol. Even where that
ordering is reversed, the feature core lowers to TPL_APPLICATION before it
walks the resource tree, which drains this callback first regardless.
Quiescing at ReadyToBoot therefore NULLs every feature driver's Redfish
service moments before that driver is asked to use it; the feature drivers
return EFI_NOT_READY without logging anything and the platform silently
provisions nothing at all - no BIOS attributes, no attribute registry, no
boot options.

So: quiesce on the feature core's own after-provisioning event when the
client feature core is present (detected by its protocol), fall back to
ReadyToBoot when it is not, and backstop both at ExitBootServices. Setup
and boot loaders still run after all of these (EfiBootManagerBoot signals
ReadyToBoot before StartImage), so the in-Setup hot-unplug window stays
covered.

Upstream-Status: Submittable [generic RedfishPkg lifecycle fix]
Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/RedfishPkg/RedfishConfigHandler/RedfishConfigHandlerCommon.c
+++ b/RedfishPkg/RedfishConfigHandler/RedfishConfigHandlerCommon.c
@@ -17,6 +17,25 @@
 EFI_EVENT                          gEndOfDxeEvent        = NULL;
 EFI_EVENT                          gExitBootServiceEvent = NULL;
 EDKII_REDFISH_CREDENTIAL_PROTOCOL  *gCredential          = NULL;
+EFI_EVENT                          gReadyToBootEvent       = NULL;
+EFI_EVENT                          gAfterProvisioningEvent = NULL;
+BOOLEAN                            gRedfishConfigQuiesced  = FALSE;
+
+//
+// RedfishClientPkg's feature core installs gEdkIIRedfishFeatureProtocolGuid
+// from its entry point, and signals gEfiRedfishClientFeatureAfterProvisioningGuid
+// as soon as RedfishFeatureDriverStartup() has finished walking the resource
+// tree. Both GUIDs are declared in RedfishClientPkg.dec and are duplicated here
+// as literals on purpose: RedfishPkg is the lower layer and must not take a
+// build dependency on RedfishClientPkg, which many platforms do not build.
+//
+STATIC EFI_GUID  mRedfishClientAfterProvisioningGuid = {
+  0xE547CB6F, 0x306F, 0x4226, { 0xAB, 0x70, 0xA0, 0x6E, 0x26, 0xF1, 0x2E, 0xD0 }
+};
+
+STATIC EFI_GUID  mRedfishClientFeatureProtocolGuid = {
+  0x785CC694, 0x4930, 0xEFBF, { 0x2A, 0xCB, 0xA4, 0xB6, 0xA1, 0xCC, 0xAA, 0x34 }
+};
 
 /**
   Callback function executed when the EndOfDxe event group is signaled.
@@ -59,6 +78,125 @@
 }
 
 /**
+  Stop every registered Redfish config handler while the network stack that
+  carries their Redfish services is still fully connected, and latch
+  gRedfishConfigQuiesced so discovery and configuration cannot restart.
+
+  Leaving the services alive into Setup or the OS is what made a hot-unplug of
+  the underlying NIC fatal (observed with a BMC USB network gadget
+  re-enumerating while the user sat in Setup, RPi5 2026-08-17): the
+  DisconnectController cascade destroys the HTTP and REST EX instances first,
+  then reaches RedfishConfigDriverBindingStop, whose handler sweep makes each
+  feature driver call RedfishCleanupService over the freed transport - ending
+  in Http->Configure through freed pool (0xAFAFAFAFAFAFAFAF fill). Stopping the
+  handlers here runs the same cleanup while every protocol instance is still
+  valid, so the later sweep from a real disconnect cascade finds only NULLed
+  services.
+
+  Idempotent: the first caller latches, the rest return immediately.
+
+  @param[in]  Reason  Human readable trigger, for the debug log.
+
+**/
+STATIC
+VOID
+RedfishConfigQuiesce (
+  IN CONST CHAR8  *Reason
+  )
+{
+  EFI_STATUS  Status;
+
+  if (gRedfishConfigQuiesced) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  gRedfishConfigQuiesced = TRUE;
+
+  Status = RedfishConfigCommonStop ();
+  DEBUG ((DEBUG_INFO, "%a: Redfish config handlers stopped (%a) - %r\n", __func__, Reason, Status));
+}
+
+/**
+  Callback function executed when RedfishClientPkg's feature core has finished
+  provisioning.
+
+  This is the normal quiesce point on any platform that builds the Redfish
+  client feature drivers: provisioning is complete, the transport is still up,
+  and Setup or the boot loader has not been started yet.
+
+  @param[in]   Event    Event whose notification function is being invoked.
+  @param[out]  Context  Pointer to the Context buffer.
+
+**/
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+RedfishConfigOnAfterProvisioning (
+  IN  EFI_EVENT  Event,
+  OUT VOID       *Context
+  )
+{
+  RedfishConfigQuiesce ("after Redfish provisioning");
+
+  //
+  // Close event, so it will not be invoked again.
+  //
+  gBS->CloseEvent (gAfterProvisioningEvent);
+  gAfterProvisioningEvent = NULL;
+}
+
+/**
+  Callback function executed when the ReadyToBoot event group is signaled.
+
+  Quiesces only when RedfishClientPkg's feature core is absent. When it is
+  present, quiescing here would break provisioning outright, so the work is
+  deferred to RedfishConfigOnAfterProvisioning().
+
+  The feature core provisions from its own ReadyToBoot callback, and both
+  callbacks sit in the same event group at the same TPL, so nothing in either
+  driver defines their relative order. In practice this one runs FIRST: the DXE
+  core queues signal-list entries with InsertHeadList, so the later-created
+  event notifies earlier, and this driver is always dispatched after the
+  feature core because of its dependency on the credential protocol. Even where
+  that ordering is reversed, the feature core lowers to TPL_APPLICATION before
+  it walks the resource tree, which drains this callback first regardless.
+
+  Quiescing here therefore NULLs every feature driver's Redfish service moments
+  before that driver is asked to use it. The feature drivers then return
+  EFI_NOT_READY without logging anything, and the platform silently provisions
+  nothing at all.
+
+  @param[in]   Event    Event whose notification function is being invoked.
+  @param[out]  Context  Pointer to the Context buffer.
+
+**/
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+RedfishConfigOnReadyToBoot (
+  IN  EFI_EVENT  Event,
+  OUT VOID       *Context
+  )
+{
+  VOID  *FeatureProtocol;
+
+  //
+  // Close event, so it will not be invoked again.
+  //
+  gBS->CloseEvent (gReadyToBootEvent);
+  gReadyToBootEvent = NULL;
+
+  if (!EFI_ERROR (gBS->LocateProtocol (&mRedfishClientFeatureProtocolGuid, NULL, &FeatureProtocol))) {
+    DEBUG ((
+      DEBUG_INFO,
+      "%a: Redfish feature core present, quiesce deferred to after-provisioning\n",
+      __func__
+      ));
+    return;
+  }
+
+  RedfishConfigQuiesce ("ReadyToBoot");
+}
+
+/**
   Callback function executed when the ExitBootService event group is signaled.
 
   @param[in]   Event    Event whose notification function is being invoked.
@@ -86,6 +224,14 @@
     }
   }
 
+  //
+  // Backstop for a platform whose feature core never reached its
+  // after-provisioning signal - it registered no resource at all, or failed
+  // before the walk. The handlers would still be holding services over network
+  // hardware the OS is about to take.
+  //
+  RedfishConfigQuiesce ("ExitBootServices");
+
   Status = gCredential->StopService (gCredential, ServiceStopTypeExitBootService);
   if (EFI_ERROR (Status) && (Status != EFI_UNSUPPORTED)) {
     DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "Redfish credential protocol failed to stop service on ExitBootService: %r", Status));
@@ -115,6 +261,16 @@
     gExitBootServiceEvent = NULL;
   }
 
+  if (gReadyToBootEvent != NULL) {
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gReadyToBootEvent);
+    gReadyToBootEvent = NULL;
+  }
+
+  if (gAfterProvisioningEvent != NULL) {
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gAfterProvisioningEvent);
+    gAfterProvisioningEvent = NULL;
+  }
+
   if (gRedfishConfigData.Event != NULL) {
     gBS->CloseEvent (gRedfishConfigData.Event);
     gRedfishConfigData.Event = NULL;
@@ -171,6 +327,51 @@
     return Status;
   }
 
+  //
+  // Create ReadyToBoot event to stop the Redfish config handlers while the
+  // network transport underneath their services is still alive.
+  //
+  Status = EfiCreateEventReadyToBootEx (
+             TPL_CALLBACK,
+             RedfishConfigOnReadyToBoot,
+             NULL,
+             &gReadyToBootEvent
+             );
+  if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gEndOfDxeEvent);
+    gEndOfDxeEvent = NULL;
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gExitBootServiceEvent);
+    gExitBootServiceEvent = NULL;
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "%a: Fail to register Ready To Boot event.", __func__));
+    return Status;
+  }
+
+  //
+  // Create the Redfish after-provisioning event. RedfishClientPkg's feature
+  // core signals it once provisioning has finished, which is the only point
+  // where the handlers can be stopped without cutting provisioning short. On a
+  // platform without the client feature drivers nothing ever signals it and
+  // the ReadyToBoot path above does the work instead.
+  //
+  Status = gBS->CreateEventEx (
+                  EVT_NOTIFY_SIGNAL,
+                  TPL_CALLBACK,
+                  RedfishConfigOnAfterProvisioning,
+                  NULL,
+                  &mRedfishClientAfterProvisioningGuid,
+                  &gAfterProvisioningEvent
+                  );
+  if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gEndOfDxeEvent);
+    gEndOfDxeEvent = NULL;
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gExitBootServiceEvent);
+    gExitBootServiceEvent = NULL;
+    gBS->CloseEvent (gReadyToBootEvent);
+    gReadyToBootEvent = NULL;
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "%a: Fail to register Redfish after-provisioning event.", __func__));
+    return Status;
+  }
+
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
 }
 
--- a/RedfishPkg/RedfishConfigHandler/RedfishConfigHandlerCommon.h
+++ b/RedfishPkg/RedfishConfigHandler/RedfishConfigHandlerCommon.h
@@ -47,6 +47,17 @@
   REDFISH_CONFIG_SERVICE_INFORMATION    RedfishServiceInfo; /// Redfish Service information discovered
 } REDFISH_CONFIG_DRIVER_DATA;
 
+//
+// TRUE once the registered Redfish config handlers have been stopped - after
+// Redfish provisioning has finished, or at ReadyToBoot on a platform that
+// builds no Redfish client feature drivers. Redfish discovery and
+// configuration must not restart past this point: the network stack underneath
+// any Redfish service can now be torn down at any time (OS driver takeover,
+// device hot-unplug), and the handlers would then free services over dead
+// transports.
+//
+extern BOOLEAN  gRedfishConfigQuiesced;
+
 /**
   Common code of unloading image for both UEFI/DXE Redfish Configuration drivers.
 
--- a/RedfishPkg/RedfishConfigHandler/RedfishConfigHandlerDriver.c
+++ b/RedfishPkg/RedfishConfigHandler/RedfishConfigHandlerDriver.c
@@ -115,6 +115,14 @@
   IN VOID       *Context
   )
 {
+  if (gRedfishConfigQuiesced) {
+    //
+    // Past ReadyToBoot: do not initialize config handlers over a network
+    // stack that can be torn down at any time from here on.
+    //
+    return;
+  }
+
   if (!gRedfishDiscoverActivated) {
     //
     // No Redfish service is discovered yet.
@@ -367,9 +375,11 @@
   gBS->CloseEvent (RedfishDiscoveredToken->Event);
 
   //
-  // Only support one Redfish service on platform.
+  // Only support one Redfish service on platform. Ignore services that
+  // complete discovery after ReadyToBoot: their transport is no longer
+  // guaranteed to outlive the handlers that would bind to them.
   //
-  if (!gRedfishServiceDiscovered) {
+  if (!gRedfishConfigQuiesced && !gRedfishServiceDiscovered) {
     RedfishInstance = RedfishDiscoveredToken->DiscoverList.RedfishInstances;
 
     // Save EFI_REDFISH_DISCOVERED_LIST for the later release.
@@ -446,6 +456,13 @@
   UINTN                                   NetworkInterfaceIndex;
   EFI_REDFISH_DISCOVERED_TOKEN            *ThisRedfishDiscoveredToken;
 
+  if (gRedfishConfigQuiesced) {
+    //
+    // Past ReadyToBoot: do not start new Redfish service discovery.
+    //
+    return;
+  }
+
   ThisNetworkInterface = mNetworkInterfaces;
   //
   // Loop to discover Redfish service on each network interface.
@@ -523,6 +540,16 @@
   EFI_HANDLE  HandleBuffer;
   VOID        *RedfishHostInterfaceReadyProtocol;
 
+  if (gRedfishConfigQuiesced) {
+    //
+    // Past ReadyToBoot: a re-installed discover protocol means the NIC was
+    // hot-unplugged and rebuilt (BMC USB gadget re-enumeration). Do not
+    // re-acquire: the cached gEfiRedfishDiscoverProtocol may point into the
+    // torn-down instance, and no configuration runs past this point anyway.
+    //
+    return;
+  }
+
   DEBUG ((DEBUG_MANAGEABILITY, "%a: New network interface is installed on system by EFI Redfish discover driver.\n", __func__));
 
   BufferSize = sizeof (EFI_HANDLE);
